  "prompt_text": "The figure shows two light-triggered nanoparticle strategies for controlling biological interactions and drug release. Overall, it illustrates how “caged” surface ligands remain inactive until exposure to light, after which they become “uncaged” and functional.\n\nIn the left portion, nanoparticles and a cell are shown with peptide ligands that initially appear blocked, preventing binding to β1 integrin on the cell surface. After illumination, the blocking groups are removed and the nanoparticles can attach to the cell, indicating phototargeting that can be switched on with spatial and temporal control. Below this cartoon, a peptide structure is paired with a photolabile protecting group; light cleaves this group to reveal the active ligand sequence, explaining the chemical basis of the targeting switch.\n\nOn the right, a gold nanocage is used as a light-responsive drug carrier. The nanocage appears coated with a shell that holds drug molecules inside. When the laser is off, the drug remains contained; when the laser is on, the coating is disrupted or opened, allowing the drug to diffuse out. Together, the panels emphasize light as an external trigger for selective cell targeting and controlled therapeutic release.",
